---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="Searched"
      eyebrow="Everything looked at, including everything that led nowhere"
      dek="An archive that publishes only its finds is advertising. This page lists every search made, what it returned, and what it cost — so that no one repeats a dead end, and so a null result can be read as evidence rather than silence.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Why a page of failures</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Most genealogical work is negative. Knowing that a register has been read line by line and does
      <em>not</em> contain a name is worth as much as finding one — provided somebody wrote it down. Nobody
      ever does, which is why the same fruitless afternoon gets spent again a decade later.</p>
    <p>So: everything below was actually looked at. Where a search failed, the reason is given — not there,
      not indexed, not digitised, restricted, or blocked. The distinction matters. A record that does not
      exist and a record nobody has scanned are different problems with different solutions.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Rosario — the baptism registers</h2>
  <p class="dek">Nuestra Señora del Rosario, the cathedral parish. Alphabetical index, DGS 4530325,
    read by eye at the film.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Covered</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Surname LERENA</strong>, every year</td><td class="m">1882–1895</td>
          <td>Nothing. Fourteen consecutive years, line by line.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Surname <strong>LLERENA</strong> (the <em>Ll</em> section)</td><td class="m">1880–1884</td>
          <td>Nothing. The whole Ll section for those five years is seven entries.</td></tr>
        <tr><td colspan="3" style="padding-top:18px"><strong>The 1882 sweep — every surname, looking for the
          given name <em>Pablo Armando</em></strong>, on the theory that a child born outside marriage was
          registered under his mother's name</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Letters <strong>A B C D E F G H I J K L Ll M N O P Q R S T U V W Y Z</strong></td>
          <td class="m">1882</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Complete</span> <strong>Every section of the index, read.
            He is not there.</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Letter <strong>X</strong></td><td class="m">—</td>
          <td>There is no X section. The index runs W straight to Y.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Letters <strong>K</strong> and <strong>Y</strong></td><td class="m">1882</td>
          <td>Contain no 1882 entries at all — K jumps 1881 to 1883; the whole Y section is one line
            for 1900.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px"><strong>Eleven near misses, every one excluded.</strong>
    <em>Chaparro · Pablo H.</em> (f.67) · <em>Funes · Armando</em> (f.163) ·
    <em>Eduardo · Paul (N.)</em> (f.505) · <em>Gorosito · Pablo</em> (1881 f.71) · a <em>Pablo J.</em>
    among the G's · a <em>Pablo</em> among the I's (1884) · <em>Pablo Eduardo</em> among the O's (f.555) ·
    a surname-only <em>Pablo · (N.)</em> among the P's · <em>Quevedo · Juan B. Pablo</em> (f.427) ·
    <em>Villarreal · Pablo del R.</em> (f.559) · and the closest of all,
    <strong><em>Ronillon · Armando Juan</em></strong> (f.498). None is him — but eleven near hits across
    718 images show the sweep catches the name when it is there, which is what makes the null result
    mean something.</div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">Useful incidental discovery: the index marks illegitimate births
    <strong>(N.)</strong>, for <em>natural</em>. And <strong>Expósito</strong> — the surname given to
    foundlings — is itself a heading in the E section, which was read.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">What the null result proves — and what it does not</h3>
  <p class="prose">The cathedral was the <strong>only parish in Rosario until 1888</strong>, and civil
    registration in Santa Fe did not begin until about 1886. So this index was the single most likely
    record of his birth anywhere. It rules out one specific thing, cleanly: <strong>no child named
    Pablo Armando was baptised at Rosario cathedral in 1882, under any surname at all.</strong></p>
  <p class="prose">It does not rule out four other possibilities, and it is worth being exact about them.
    He may have been baptised under a <strong>different given name</strong> — he was <em>Robert Paul</em>
    in South Africa and <em>Pablo Armando</em> only on the passport, and the sweep looked for that pair.
    He may have been baptised in a <strong>different year</strong>, if the passport's 22 February 1882 was
    wrong or approximate. He may have been born <strong>somewhere else entirely</strong> and only later
    associated with Rosario. Or he may never have been entered in this register at all.</p>
  <p class="prose">The second of those is now the cheapest thing left to test. Every letter's position in
    the film is mapped, so running the same sweep across <strong>1881 and 1883</strong> would cost a
    fraction of what 1882 cost.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Rosario — everything else</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Search</th><th>Result</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Marriage index</strong> 1860–1904, L section, image 51</td>
          <td>A line reading <em>"Larena V · Sarmiento A · 20"</em> under Libro 9, 1884</td>
          <td><span class="chip inf">Unresolved</span> Cannot be distinguished from <em>Lavena</em>, a real
            Rosario surname. Needs the register at folio 20.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Other Rosario parishes</strong> for 1882</td>
          <td>There were none</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Closed</span> Santa Rosa de Lima became a parish on 19 February 1888
            and performed its first baptism that day. Until then the city had only the cathedral.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Santa Fe provincial civil registration</strong>, 1882</td>
          <td>Did not exist</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Closed</span> Argentine civil registration began around 1886. For an
            1882 birth the parish register is the only record that could ever have existed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Control search: <strong>surname López at Rosario, births 1888–92</strong></td>
          <td>Zero results</td>
          <td>Proves the cathedral's 19th-century baptisms are <strong>not indexed</strong> — they can only
            be read, never searched.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Indexes and databases searched</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Searched for</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>FamilySearch</strong>, worldwide</td><td>Pablo Armando Lerena, fuzzy, births 1878–86</td>
          <td><strong>Two records exist on earth</strong>: his 1911 Cape marriage and a modern Find a Grave
            memorial. Nothing from South America at all.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FamilySearch</td><td>Given name <em>Pablo Armando</em>, any surname, Argentina</td>
          <td>Nothing born 1880–84.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FamilySearch</td><td>Surname Lerena, filtered to <strong>Uruguay</strong></td>
          <td>Nothing in the birth window.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FamilySearch</td><td>Surname Lerena, <strong>military records</strong></td>
          <td><strong>Zero.</strong> The Argentine army collection is not indexed for this name — so the
            <em>libreta de enrolamiento</em> must be requested by letter, not searched.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Argentine National Census 1895</strong></td><td>Surname Lerena</td>
          <td>A Uruguayan-born household in Buenos Aires — <strong>not his</strong> — and
            <strong>not one Lerena in Santa Fe</strong>. He is absent though he would have been thirteen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>NAAIRS</strong> (South African National Archives)</td><td>LERENA, all repositories</td>
          <td>13 documents, 12 genuine. <a href={u("/naairs/")}>The archives answer →</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td>NAAIRS</td><td><strong>LLERENA</strong></td><td><strong>Zero.</strong> No spelling variant to chase in South Africa.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>NAAIRS</td><td>Gravestones database (GEN)</td><td><strong>Zero</strong> Lerena gravestones indexed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FamilySearch</td><td>Cape <strong>birth registrations</strong> for the three sons</td>
          <td>Not indexed. Would have given the father's birthplace three times over.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Deeds registers</strong>, South Africa</td><td>Lerena land</td>
          <td>No land for Pablo Armando; two suburban plots for his son Nuno. The family says he held land
            at Blouberg — so the search, not the claim, is probably at fault.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Blocked, restricted, or otherwise unreachable</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>CEMLA</strong> — Argentine immigrant arrivals, Buenos Aires<br>
            <span class="cap">Lerena · Llerena · Larena · Lerina, forename blank, 1800–1960</span></td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Done</span> Run 9 Sep 2026, David completing the CAPTCHAs at the
            keyboard. <strong>All four spellings returned hits and all are recorded</strong> on the
            <a href="/TheLerena/register/">name register</a>. The decisive result is negative: the
            <strong>earliest arrival under Lerena, Llerena or Larena is 1905</strong>, so his parents
            cannot be in this index — they were in Argentina before it becomes useful. Kept for what it
            did turn up: an <strong>Argentine Gilberto Lerena born about 1879</strong>, and a
            Uruguayan <strong>Carlos Lerena born about 1885</strong> travelling with an Argentine
            wife.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>South Africa, Cape probate records</strong> on FamilySearch</td>
          <td><em>"Image Restricted"</em> — viewable only at an affiliate library. This is how the estate
            pages already held were presumably obtained.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>LDS South African Mission census, 1935</strong></td>
          <td>Both younger sons are indexed as <em>"Son"</em> — so a head of household stands on that page,
            giving his own birthplace five years before the passport. <strong>The image is not
            published.</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>UK outward passenger lists (BT 27)</strong></td>
          <td>Subscription only (FindMyPast, Ancestry). Not reachable from here.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sporting Post</strong> and <strong>PressReader</strong></td>
          <td>Return 403 / bot-verification to automated access. The Alan Higgins obituary naming
            <em>"Bob Lerena"</em> was supplied as a PDF instead.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Forebears</strong>, Spain sub-page</td>
          <td>Returns empty to automated fetch. Resolved another way — the main surname page carries the
            regional split in prose.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Chased and abandoned</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Lead</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>The San Miguel baptisms of 1872</strong> — Roberto Juan Roque <em>Llorens</em>, son
          of Roberto Llorens and Francisca Fernández</td>
          <td><span class="chip lore">Unproven</span> David Defranceski states plainly these are not
            established as this family's. The surname is <em>Llorens</em>, a Catalan name; the streets and
            parish point to <strong>Buenos Aires</strong>, not Rosario. Kept in the project's
            <span class="mono">sources/unproven/</span> so it is not chased twice.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>The <strong>Fernández</strong> hypothesis that grew from it</td>
          <td><span class="chip dis">Failed</span> The F section of 1882 is read in full. No Pablo Armando.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>"Ler · María · 1884 · folio 13"</strong>, an odd truncated surname in the index</td>
          <td><span class="chip dis">Does not resolve</span> Stamped folios 11, 13 and 14 of Libro 27 all
            read; no <em>Ler</em>, no matching María. The index's foliation runs about two ahead of the
            book's, and applying that offset did not find it either. Dropped.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>"Larena/Lavena V × Sarmiento A"</strong>, marriage index 1884</td>
          <td><span class="chip inf">Open</span> Cannot be read with confidence at the best resolution
            available. Settle at DGS 4531041, Libro 9 folio 20.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A <strong>second Rosario parish</strong> holding the baptism</td>
          <td><span class="chip dis">Closed</span> Reasonable for a day; wrong. There was no second parish
            until 1888.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Find a Grave memorial for Pablo Armando</td>
          <td>User-created in April 2025, <em>burial details unknown</em>, and its Rosario derives from the
            same passport. <strong>Not independent corroboration.</strong> It does compute his age at death
            as 68, confirming the death certificate's "76" is wrong.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">One file in the family's Lerena folder — a Brisbane cemetery index
    listing FALCO burials — is <strong>misfiled</strong> and belongs to the sibling Falco archive.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>What the failures add up to</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Three independent bodies of evidence decline to place this family at Rosario: fourteen years of
      cathedral baptisms, the whole FamilySearch index, and the 1895 national census. And there is no civil
      fallback, because provincial registration did not yet exist.</p>
    <p>That is not nothing. It means <strong>his South American origin rests entirely on his own
      passport</strong> — a document he swore at a consulate, naming a city, a province and a day. The
      record has not contradicted him. It has simply, so far, refused to agree.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">If you have looked somewhere this page does not list, that is
    worth knowing too. Corrections and additions are the most valuable thing anyone can send.</div>
</section>
</Base>
